./app/work/observer_ingest_min/generated/split_units_youtube_01_29_20260322_172245.json:7:    "text_excerpt": "## 2025년 코딩의 해를 지나 2026년 과학의 해로 노정석: 녹화를 하고 있는 오늘은 2025년 12월 21일 일요일 아침입니다. 네, 2025년이 이제 저물어 가고 있는데요. 2025년에 가장 큰 사건을 꼽으라면 아무래도 Claude Code 출시로 시작된 코딩의 해였을 겁니다. 2026년은 코딩은 이미 끝난 일이고 이제 과학이 또 끝나는 그런 한 해가 될 것이다, AI에 의해서. 이",
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:9:Widespread adoption of open source agents is here. OpenClaw and others run about your computer. Continuous run harnesses like Claude Code and Codex (using ECC) increase the surface area; and on February 25, 2026, Check Point Research published a Claude Code disclosure that should have ended the "this could happen but won't / is overblown" phase of the conversation for good. With the tooling reaching critical mass, the gravity of exploits multiplies.
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:31:GitHub PR reviews are another target. Malicious instructions can live in hidden diff comments, issue bodies, linked docs, tool output, even "helpful" review context. If you have upstream bots set up (code review agents, Greptile, Cubic, etc.) or use downstream local automated approaches (OpenClaw, Claude Code, Codex, Copilot coding agent, whatever it is); with low oversight and high autonomy in reviewing PRs, you are increasing your surface area risk of getting prompt injected AND affecting every user downstream of your repo with the exploit.
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:41:## Claude Code CVEs (February 2026)
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:43:Check Point Research published the Claude Code findings on February 25, 2026. The issues were reported between July and December 2025, then patched before publication.
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:49:> Hijacking Claude Code users via poisoned config files with rogue hooks actions.
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:54:> _I hacked Claude Code! It turns out "agentic" is just a fancy new way to get a shell. I achieved full RCE and hijacked organization API keys. CVE-2025-59536 | CVE-2026-21852_
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:71:Claude Code had its repo-controlled hooks, MCP settings, and env-var trust paths tested publicly. Amazon Q Developer had a 2025 supply chain incident involving a malicious prompt payload in the VS Code extension, then a separate disclosure around overly broad GitHub token exposure in build infrastructure. Weak credential boundaries plus agent-adjacent tooling is an entrypoint for opportunists.
./references/git_search/everything-claude-code-main/the-security-guide.md:101:| **CVSS 8.7** | Claude Code hook / pre-trust execution issue: CVE-2025-59536 |
